package org.example.language_fundamentals_01;

public class ConstructorChaining {

    /*
    🌐 What Is Constructor Chaining?

    - Constructor chaining is when one constructor calls another constructor
      in the same class (this(...)) or the superclass (super(...)).
    - In an inheritance hierarchy, constructor chaining ensures that
      the parent class is initialized before the child.


    🧬 Our Class Setup Overview

    We will build three classes:

    - P1:
      Has three constructors:
      no-arg, two-arg, and var-arg.

    - C1 extends P1:
      Calls one of the P1 constructors using super(...).

    - GC1 extends C1:
      Calls a C1 constructor, continuing the chain.


    💡 Code Example: Constructor Chaining with Overloading


    class P1 {

        P1() {
            System.out.println("Parent: no-arg constructor");
        }

        P1(int a, int b) {
            System.out.println("Parent: 2-arg constructor → "
                    + a + ", " + b);
        }

        P1(int... values) {
            System.out.println("Parent: var-arg constructor → "
                    + java.util.Arrays.toString(values));
        }
    }


    class C1 extends P1 {

        C1() {
            // Calls Parent's no-arg constructor
            super();

            System.out.println("Child: no-arg constructor");
        }

        C1(int x, int y) {
            // Calls Parent's 2-arg constructor
            super(x, y);

            System.out.println("Child: 2-arg constructor → "
                    + x + ", " + y);
        }

        C1(int... nums) {
            // Calls Parent's var-arg constructor
            super(nums);

            System.out.println("Child: var-arg constructor → "
                    + java.util.Arrays.toString(nums));
        }
    }


    class GC1 extends C1 {

        GC1() {
            // Chaining:
            // Child's no-arg -> Parent's no-arg

            super();

            System.out.println("Grandchild: no-arg constructor");
        }

        GC1(int a, int b) {
            // Chaining:
            // Child's 2-arg -> Parent's 2-arg

            super(a, b);

            System.out.println("Grandchild: 2-arg constructor");
        }

        GC1(int... values) {
            // Chaining:
            // Child's var-arg -> Parent's var-arg

            super(values);

            System.out.println("Grandchild: var-arg constructor");
        }
    }


    🧪 Sample Output (When Instantiating)


    new GC1();

    Output:

    Parent: no-arg constructor
    Child: no-arg constructor
    Grandchild: no-arg constructor


    new GC1(5,10);

    Output:

    Parent: 2-arg constructor → 5, 10
    Child: 2-arg constructor → 5, 10
    Grandchild: 2-arg constructor


    new GC1(1,2,3);

    Output:

    Parent: var-arg constructor → [1, 2, 3]
    Child: var-arg constructor → [1, 2, 3]
    Grandchild: var-arg constructor


    🎯 Why This Matters

    - Ensures objects are constructed top-down:
      superclass → subclass.

    - Helps enforce initialization order.

    - Provides control over which parent constructor
      is triggered by subclass constructors.


    🆕 Java 17 Additions Related to Constructor Chaining


    📦 Records (Java 16+, standard in Java 17)

    NEW constructor model with different rules.


    Example:

    record Point(int x, int y) {

        // Compact constructor:
        // Runs BEFORE fields are assigned.

        // NO explicit this(...) or super(...) call allowed here.

        // Implicit super() to Object happens automatically.

        Point {

            if(x < 0 || y < 0)
                throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "negative coords");
        }


        // Overloaded constructor:
        // MUST delegate to canonical constructor.

        Point(int x) {

            this(x,x);
        }
    }


    Record Rules:

    - Records are implicitly final.
    - Records can extend only Object.
    - No parent-class constructor chaining possible.
    - Canonical constructor is auto-generated.
    - Compact constructor cannot use this(...) or super(...).
    - Additional constructors must eventually call canonical constructor
      using this(...).


    🔒 Sealed Classes (Java 17)

    Constructor chaining rules remain unchanged,
    only inheritance hierarchy is restricted.


    Example:


    sealed class P1 permits C1 {

        P1() {
            System.out.println("Parent: no-arg constructor");
        }
    }


    final class C1 extends P1 {

        C1() {

            super();

            System.out.println("Child: no-arg constructor");
        }
    }


    Rules:

    - Sealed classes restrict which classes can extend a class.
    - super(...) and this(...) rules remain unchanged.
    - Every permitted subclass follows normal
      top-down constructor chaining.


    🔡 var with Constructor Calls (Java 10+)


    Example:

    var gc1 = new GC1(1,2,3);


    - var only provides local variable type inference.
    - It has zero effect on constructor chaining.


    🚫 Deprecated Related To This Topic


    Wrapper constructors are deprecated since Java 9.


    Avoid:

    Integer i = new Integer(10);

    Boolean b = new Boolean(true);


    Use:

    Integer i = Integer.valueOf(10);

    Integer i = 10; // autoboxing


    Reason:

    - Wrapper objects are immutable.
    - Factory methods use caching.
    - Avoid unnecessary object creation.


    Important:

    valueOf(...) is NOT constructor chaining.

    Only constructors participate in:

    this(...)
    super(...)


    📝 Quick Summary


    Normal Class (P1/C1/GC1):

    Supports:
    this()/super()

    Notes:
    Fully flexible.


    Record:

    Supports:
    Yes, restricted.

    Notes:
    Overloaded constructor must call canonical constructor.


    Sealed Class:

    Supports:
    Yes.

    Notes:
    Only inheritance is restricted.


    var:

    Supports:
    N/A

    Notes:
    Only type inference.


    Deprecated Wrapper Constructor:

    Supports:
    N/A

    Notes:
    Avoid, use factory methods.

    */

}